                                    </td>
                                </tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                    </div>
                    <p>
                        A Custom Metadata row does not automatically make Flow invoke any arbitrary Flow API name. Use supported, explicitly configured branches/subflows. “No Apex per use case” is feasible for work supported by Flow and available actions; it is not a promise that all parsing, concurrency, or integration requirements can be handled without code.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        <strong>Proposed additions — none of these fields or objects are in the verified inventory:</strong>
                    </p>
                    <ul>
                        <li>
                            Submission source, business unit, and schema version for multi-source contracts and migrations.
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            Processing completion time, attempt count, next retry time, and a structured reason code for operations.
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            A child Marketing Submission Action object with parent submission, action key, status, attempts, next retry, and target record ID.
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            A unique action execution key derived from submission identity plus a stable action identifier. This separates event replay protection from individual outcome replay protection.
                        </li>
                    </ul>
                    <p>
                        For example, one submission could create a Task and add a Campaign Member. Each action should record its own success, so retrying a failed campaign update does not recreate the successful Task. Make new behavior opt-in through approved configuration; old submissions should not acquire new side effects merely because configuration changed.
                    </p>
                    <h2 id="scaling-and-operational-controls">
                        Scaling and operational controls
                    </h2>
                    <p>
                        This is a scalable foundation because ingestion is small, source records survive downstream failures, and processing can evolve independently. It is not an unlimited-throughput or exactly-once guarantee. Establish expected peak volume, latency, and retention, then test against this org&#x27;s actual automation and limits.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        <strong>Capture and consistency.</strong> Validate configured values and use a real source event key when one exists. Otherwise retain invocation-level GUIDs and document the replay limitation. Preserve captured facts, restrict edits to snapshot fields, and handle duplicate-key races explicitly. Avoid overwriting submissions through External ID upserts. A unique submission key alone does not guarantee unique Tasks.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        <strong>Identity and timing.</strong> CRM ID takes precedence; blank-ID email fallback uses Contact first and LastModifiedDate descending. New/Existing reflects original-ID presence, not CRM age. Active V7 retains unmatched submissions; later sync still needs explicit reconciliation/retry. Historical V6 and V3 skipped unmatched events. Do not depend on completion-action ordering or assume that HML preserves form-submit-time identity.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        <strong>Processing performance.</strong> Capture uses one Contact query and a Lead query only when needed, each returning one ID; it uses no loops. The existing event-key lookup still applies. Keep database writes out of loops, use selective filters, and test batches as well as individual interviews. Verified object indexes include Submission Key, Lead, Contact, Owner, and Submission Number. Form Key and Processing Status are currently shown as not indexed. Assess query selectivity and latency against actual Contact/Lead volume; avoid scanning payload text.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        <strong>Failure recovery.</strong> Implement bounded retries with delay, an exhausted-retry review path, and monitoring for stale Received and Pending CRM Match records. Capture actionable error details. Avoid blind status resets and concurrent manual retries. Source ingestion and downstream processing are separate failure boundaries; monitor both.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        <strong>Sales access and responsibility.</strong> Define who owns submissions versus Tasks, confirm queue membership, and verify Sales access to both submission history and the related person/account. Test the actual integration execution identity and least-privilege permissions. Queue assignment alone is not a notification or SLA process.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        <strong>Reporting and retention.</strong> Track captured submissions by asset and CreatedDate; use source time only when supplied reliably. V7 retains unmatched invocations when identity is supplied, but still excludes fully blank identity and replay skips. V6/V3 counts also exclude no-match invocations. Use MCAE activity plus version-aware traces to reconcile gaps. Track pending/failed volume, age, routing latency, and outcomes for stored records. Follow Up Task ID is text, so cross-object Task reporting needs an explicit report/data design. Agree retention and archive rules for personal data and captured details; do not log credentials.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        <strong>Versioning.</strong> Version the input contract and configuration when meanings change. Preserve compatibility for existing forms. Maintain Flow metadata and configuration in source control with a repeatable deployment and rollback plan.
                    </p>
